<Page
  title="How Waymark works: a study plan built around your Life in the UK test date"
  description="Waymark plans your Life in the UK revision around your test date, brings back facts just before you forget them, and gives you timed mock exams and an honest readiness estimate."
  heading="How Waymark works"
  lead="A short daily plan, built around your test date, that keeps bringing back what you're about to forget."
>
  <h2>1. Tell us your test date</h2>
  <p>
    Pick your test date, or choose "not booked yet", and say how long you can study each day: 10, 20 or 30 minutes.
    Waymark spreads the whole handbook across the days you have, so you have seen every topic at least five days before
    your test.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Take a quick check</h2>
  <p>
    Answer 24 questions across all five chapters. It takes about 10 minutes and shows where you already know the
    material and where to start.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Follow your daily plan</h2>
  <p>
    Each day you get a short session of reviews and new facts. Facts you get wrong come back soon; facts you know come
    back less often. This is called spaced repetition. Waymark uses a modern scheduling method (FSRS) and never
    schedules a review for after your test.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Practise under test conditions</h2>
  <p>
    Mock exams match the real format: 24 questions, 45 minutes, and you need 18 correct to pass. You can flag questions
    and go back, and you only see your answers at the end. Then every answer comes with a plain-English explanation and
    the handbook chapter and section it comes from.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Check your readiness, honestly</h2>
  <p>
    Waymark estimates how ready you are from your first-attempt answers on questions you haven't just seen. It shows a
    band (Not yet, Borderline, Likely to pass) only once it has enough evidence: 150 different questions and two timed
    mocks. It is an estimate from your practice, never a guarantee.
  </p>

  <h2>Every answer checked</h2>
  <p>
    All questions are written in our own words from the official handbook, <em>Life in the United Kingdom: A Guide for
    New Residents</em> (3rd edition). Each one names the chapter and section it comes from, so you can check it in your
    own copy. If something looks wrong, you can report it from the question, and every fix is listed in our
    <a href="/corrections">corrections log</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Your data stays with you</h2>
  <p>
    You don't need an account. Your progress is stored on your own device, and there is no advertising or tracking.
    See our <a href="/privacy">privacy notice</a>.
  </p>

  <p><a class="btn" href="/app">Start studying free</a></p>
</Page>
